// Stage-2 port of `runtime/root.zig` (#1367): the ABI surface itself — the
// `bit_rt_*` symbols codegen calls by fixed name, and the process-wide
// singletons they run on. Both implementations exist during the port; #1369
// swaps this one into `libbitrt.a` and retires the Zig original.
//
// THIS FILE IS A PARTIAL PORT, AND THE BOUNDARY IS DELIBERATE. What is here is
// the whole of root's *managed-data* surface: the string, slice, map and
// interface object models, which together are the layouts every compiled Bit
// program manipulates on every line. What is NOT here is listed in "SEAMS"
// below, each blocked on a named, still-unported dependency rather than on
// effort. A half-built subsystem would be worse than a missing one: the ABI is
// a contract, and a symbol that exists but is wrong is indistinguishable from a
// correct one until a program miscompiles.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM THE ZIG, AND WHY (the complete list)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. STATE IS MODULE STATE, NOT A CALLER-OWNED BLOCK. This is the first port to
//    use §11.11 module-level `let`, and it is the reason #1402 existed: root's
//    singletons ARE the globalness that #1362/#1363/#1364 each pushed out to
//    their caller. Every cell below is a scalar or a fixed array of scalars, so
//    rule 1 (the collector never scans module state) holds by construction —
//    and it must, because `heapBlock`'s free-list heads point into unmanaged
//    span memory that carries no object header.
//
// 2. TYPE DESCRIPTORS ARE BUILT AT RUNTIME, NOT AT COMPTIME. Zig writes
//    `TypeInfo.of(...)` as a comptime constant. A module-state initializer must
//    be a compile-time constant and an array-typed `let` takes none (§11.11
//    rule 2), so the six descriptors are filled once by `rootInit`. Their
//    CONTENTS are identical field for field; only the moment of writing moves.
//    `refArrayInfo` in particular is matched by ADDRESS by the collector, so it
//    must be a stable cell — which module state is.
//
// 3. OUT-OF-MEMORY RETURNS 0 INSTEAD OF PANICKING. `@nosplit` cannot call
//    `panic` (it is an out-of-line call that can allocate and reach a
//    safepoint), the same constraint `runtime/chan` documents for send-on-closed
//    and `runtime/alloc` for its dropped assertions. Every allocating entry
//    point here returns 0 on exhaustion and bumps `statOom`, so exhaustion stays
//    VISIBLE (unlike Zig's asserts, which compile out of the shipping build)
//    rather than silent. Raising it as a real panic belongs to the boot layer
//    that owns the write path — see SEAM 3.
//
// 4. PAGES COME FROM A CALLER-SUPPLIED RESERVE. Unchanged from #1362/#1363 and
//    forced the same way: `syscall` is Linux-only, `extern function` is
//    Darwin-only, and both are rejected on the DECLARATION even when never
//    called, so a platform-free core cannot map its own pages. `rootBindMemory`
//    takes the reserve; binding a platform provider to it is SEAM 3's job.
//
// 5. ADDRESSES ARE SIGNED `int`. Bit's `int` is i64 (§11.1), as in every other
//    runtime module here. Userspace addresses on all three supported targets are
//    far below 2^63, so every comparison orders identically to Zig's `usize`.
//
// 6. `bit_rt_iface_as_ok`'s FLAG IS PROCESS-WIDE, NOT PER-THREAD. Zig uses a
//    `threadlocal`. `@threadlocal` is rejected for Mach-O at emission (§11.11),
//    and aarch64-macos is a required target, so a per-thread cell cannot carry
//    it portably. This is SOUND ONLY UNDER THE ADJACENCY CONTRACT the ABI
//    already states for it (§2.2): lowering emits `iface_as` and `iface_as_ok`
//    back to back with no yield between, so no second assertion can land in the
//    window. It is nonetheless a WEAKER guarantee than the Zig's under real
//    multi-threaded mutation, and the honest fix is per-task scratch — see
//    SEAM 4. Flagged rather than hidden.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SEAMS — what this file deliberately does not define, and what blocks each
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// SEAM 1 — THE NETWORK (12 symbols: `bit_rt_net_listen/local_port/accept/dial/
//   read/write/udp_bind/udp_send/udp_recv/udp_sender_host/udp_sender_port/
//   resolve`). `runtime/net.zig` is UNPORTED (#1366). root's net wrappers are
//   pure marshalling — every one is `net.<call>() catch return -1` — so they are
//   a mechanical half-hour ONCE net exists and are unwritable before then. They
//   also need the netpoller, which is itself blocked on #1431. Nothing here
//   references net, so wiring them later adds symbols without touching a line
//   of this file.
//
// SEAM 2 — THE STOP-THE-WORLD REGISTRY (`bit_rt_gc_thread_enter/thread_exit/
//   blocking_begin/blocking_end`, and the safepoint handshake). `gc.zig`'s
//   `World`/`Mutator` (gc.zig:176-350 — `claim`, `release`, `park`,
//   `stopRequested`, `beginBlocking`, `rendezvous`, `restart`,
//   `forEachParkedFrame`) is NOT part of the #1363 port: `runtime/gc/gc.bit`
//   exports the collector core only, and mentions `World` nowhere outside
//   comments. These four symbols are thin wrappers over exactly that registry,
//   so they cannot be written before it exists. Porting `World` is a task-sized
//   piece of work in its own right (it is the multi-thread rendezvous, the part
//   #1429 just rebuilt), not a subroutine of this one.
//
// SEAM 3 — BOOT AND PROCESS ENTRY. **THE WRITE/EXIT HALF IS NOW BUILT**, in
//   `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/io.bit`: `bit_rt_panic`, `bit_rt_assert`,
//   `bit_rt_print`, `bit_rt_eprint` and `bit_rt_os_exit` (pinned with this
//   file's `bit_rt_root_` prefix, same rename obligation), over a shared
//   `writeAll`/`panicWrite` pair. Linux takes raw `write`/`exit_group` syscalls
//   (§11.8), Darwin libSystem `write`/`_exit` (§11.7), which is why they are two
//   files: the two primitives cannot appear in one module. Verified by
//   tests/stress/rootio{,linux,darwin} on all three targets.
//
//   WHAT REMAINS IN THIS SEAM: `_start`, `machoMain`, `boot`, and the fs/os/time
//   primitives. `boot` is the substantial piece — it sequences heap, collector,
//   scheduler and the first green thread, and startup ORDERING is load-bearing.
//
//   AND A CONSTRAINT THE WRITE HALF SURFACED, which #1369 must plan around:
//   **nothing in a platform provider can be `@nosplit`, because `@nosplit`
//   rejects `syscall`** (E0075, measured). A freestanding archive member must be
//   `@nosplit` (§10.3), so as things stand the write path — and therefore any
//   fully-Bit `libbitrt.a` — cannot be freestanding. It is the same shape as the
//   `ptrOf` gap #1421 is fixing: §10.3.1 admits forms that "lower to inline
//   machine instructions rather than a call", and §11.8 says the backends emit
//   the kernel trap inline, so `syscall` meets §10.3.1's own criterion and is
//   simply missing from its allowlist. See `linux/io.bit`'s header for the full
//   argument. Every syscall-using provider in this tree is non-`@nosplit` today,
//   so this is a pre-existing property of the port, not a regression from it.
//
// SEAM 4 — PER-GOROUTINE SLOTS (`bit_rt_set_err`/`get_err`,
//   `bit_rt_chan_recv_ok`). Zig carries these in `threadlocal`s, which Mach-O
//   refuses. The right home is PER-TASK SCRATCH at the base of the running
//   task's own stack — the mechanism `runtime/sched`'s `schedCurrentTask` and
//   `runtime/chan`'s `scratchOf` already establish, and strictly stronger than a
//   per-worker threadlocal because it survives task migration. Doing it means
//   extending `chanScratchWords`' layout, which is a SHARED contract with
//   `runtime/chan`; #1431 is editing that neighbourhood in parallel and a
//   drive-by change to a shared layout is exactly how two agents produce a
//   defect that exists only in the combination of their work. Deferred on
//   purpose, with the mechanism already identified.
//
// SEAM 5 — FLOAT TEXT AND THE TRANSCENDENTALS (`bit_rt_string_from_float`,
//   `bit_rt_parse_float`, `bit_rt_pow/atan2/log/log2/log10`). These are not
//   marshalling: Zig's `{d}` is a shortest-round-trip (Ryu-class) formatter and
//   `parseFloat` is a correctly-rounded decimal->binary conversion. A 1-ULP
//   drift in either FLIPS THE PRINTED STRING, and byte-identical `const_float`
//   IR dumps are a self-hosting gate — so an approximate port would fail the
//   gates loudly at best and corrupt a compiler's float constants at worst.
//   Porting them is a numerics task with its own test vectors.
//
//   AND THE ROUNDING SIX ARE NOT THE EASY HALF OF THIS SEAM. An earlier attempt
//   split `floor/ceil/round/trunc/sqrt/float32Bits` off as "one-liners over
//   primitives that already lower to the instruction". They do not lower to the
//   instruction — they lower to a call to the very symbol being ported. That is
//   SEAM 6 below, and it is a different problem from the 1-ULP one: not "hard to
//   get bit-exact" but "cannot be expressed in Bit at all".

// SYMBOL PINS CARRY A `bit_rt_root_` PREFIX, NOT THE REAL ABI NAMES.
//
// `runtime/root.zig` is still linked into every binary and already defines
// `bit_rt_map_new`, `bit_rt_slice_get` and the rest; pinning those names here
// makes every link fail with `DuplicateSymbol` (measured, not predicted). The
// pins still have to exist — `-O1` splices callee bodies into their callers, so
// the freestanding emit (#1400) resolves by symbol and refuses on anything
// unpinned — so they are prefixed instead of dropped.
//
// #1369 OWNS THE RENAME: when it retires `root.zig`, each pin below drops its
// `_root` infix and claims the real ABI name. The prefix is a link-time
// placeholder, not a second ABI, and no other module should call these by name.
// `runtime/alloc` is pinned the same way for a different reason — its names
// were never in the ABI to begin with (ABI.md names no allocator symbol).

import { loadWord, storeWord, loadByte, storeByte, wordAt } from "../gc"
import {
  gcInit, gcBindMemory, gcConfigure, gcAlloc, gcAllocRaw, gcOwns, gcWords,
  gcTiSize, gcTiPtrOffsetsPtr, gcTiPtrOffsetsLen, gcTiNamePtr, gcTiNameLen,
  gcTiMethodsPtr, gcTiMethodsLen, gcHeaderSize, gcHdrInfo,
} from "../gc"
import { heapInit, heapWords } from "../alloc"
import { spinUnlocked } from "../"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Object layouts (ABI.md §2) — reproduced field for field from `root.zig`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A `string` value: a GC object holding `{ptr, len}` immediately followed by its
// bytes, with `ptr` addressing those inline bytes. The object is therefore a
// LEAF — `ptr` is an interior pointer into the object itself, never a separate
// reference — which is why `stringInfo` carries no pointer offsets. This is also
// why `bit_rt_string_slice` COPIES instead of sharing: an interior pointer
// cannot keep a backing object alive (ABI.md §3).
export const strPtr: int = 0
export const strLen: int = 8
export const strHeaderSize: int = 16

// A `[]T` value: `{buf, len, off, cap, is_ref}`. `buf` is a SEPARATE GC object
// (the header's one traced reference, hence ptr_offsets = [0]) holding
// `off + cap` words; the view is `buf[off .. off+len]`. A reslice shares `buf`
// and only moves `off`/`len`/`cap`, so `buf` stays an object BASE and no
// interior pointer is ever stored as a reference. `len` sits at +8, the same
// offset as the string header's, so one `slice_len` serves both.
export const slcBuf: int = 0
export const slcLen: int = 8
export const slcOff: int = 16
export const slcCap: int = 24
export const slcIsRef: int = 32
export const slcHeaderSize: int = 40

// A `map<K,V>`: three parallel `cap`-slot buffers plus the bookkeeping. `keys`,
// `vals` and `ctrl` are all traced bases (ptr_offsets = [0, 8, 16]) — `ctrl` is
// a leaf buffer but must still be KEPT ALIVE, which only a traced slot does.
export const mapKeys: int = 0
export const mapVals: int = 8
export const mapCtrl: int = 16
export const mapLen: int = 24
export const mapCap: int = 32
export const mapUsed: int = 40
export const mapKeyIsString: int = 48
export const mapValIsRef: int = 56
export const mapHeaderSize: int = 64

// Per-slot state. Linear probing with tombstones; the 7/8 load-factor grow
// below guarantees an EMPTY slot always exists, which is what makes every probe
// loop terminate in at most `cap` steps.
const ctrlEmpty: int = 0
const ctrlFull: int = 1
const ctrlTomb: int = 2

// The initial slot count. Power of two, so `& (cap - 1)` is the modulus.
const mapInitCap: int = 8

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Process-wide singletons (§11.11) — root.zig's `g_*`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every cell is a scalar or a fixed array of scalars: rule 1 holds, so the
// collector never scans any of it. That is REQUIRED here, not merely allowed —
// `heapBlock` holds 37 free-list heads pointing into unmanaged mmap span memory
// with no object header, and walking one as a reference would decode arbitrary
// bytes as a GC header.
//
// Sizes are literals because a module-state array's length must be a literal,
// not a `const` (measured: `let a: [N]i64` is E0021). `rootInit` asserts each
// against the exported width it stands for, so a drift in `gcWords`/`heapWords`
// fails loudly at boot instead of corrupting memory.
let gcState: [37]i64          // gcWords
let heapBlock: [39]i64        // heapWords
let heapLockCell: i32 = 0
let listLockCell: i32 = 0

// The six type descriptors, plus the two pointer-offset arrays they name.
// See `rootInit` for the layout; 7 words is one `TypeInfo` (ABI.md §2).
let infoBlock: [46]i64

const riString: int = 0
const riSlice: int = 7
const riSliceBuf: int = 14
const riMap: int = 21
const riMapCtrl: int = 28
const riRefArray: int = 35
const riSliceOffs: int = 42   // [0]
const riMapOffs: int = 43     // [0, 8, 16]
const riWords: int = 46

// The addresses of `gcState` and `infoBlock`, taken ONCE by `rootInit`.
//
// THIS INDIRECTION IS FORCED, and the reason matters. `ptrOf` is REJECTED in a
// `@nosplit` body (E0075, measured) even on module state, which SPEC §11.11
// explicitly says is legal there ("`ptrOf` yields its address for the atomic
// builtins ... legal inside a `@nosplit` body"). Filed as a spec/implementation
// mismatch rather than worked around silently.
//
// And `@nosplit` is NOT optional for the bodies below, it is the correctness
// property: they hold live object addresses in plain `int` locals, which the
// stack maps do NOT describe as references (ABI.md §4 names only reference-typed
// slots). A safepoint firing inside `rtStringConcat` would therefore find no
// root for the string it has just allocated and sweep it. `@nosplit` is what
// guarantees no safepoint lands there — so the addresses must be reachable
// WITHOUT `ptrOf`, i.e. as plain module-state reads plus `wordAt`.
let gcAddr: i64 = 0
let infoAddr: i64 = 0

let booted: i64 = 0

// The `ok` of the most recent `bit_rt_iface_as`. Process-wide — see deviation 6.
let ifaceOk: i64 = 0

// Allocation failures observed since boot. Zig makes OOM fatal; `@nosplit`
// cannot, so it is counted instead and every allocating entry point returns 0.
// A non-zero value means some caller got a null it may not have checked.
let statOom: i64 = 0

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Boot
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Writes one `TypeInfo` (ABI.md §2) at word `at` of `infoBlock`.
//
// `name` is carried as an address/length pair exactly as Zig's is; a descriptor
// with no name passes 0/0. Names are only ever read by
// `bit_rt_iface_assert`'s message, so a nameless descriptor degrades that
// message rather than misbehaving.
@nosplit function writeInfo(base: int, at: int, size: int, offsAddr: int, offsLen: int, nameAddr: int, nameLen: int) {
  let w = base + at * 8
  storeWord(w + gcTiSize, size)
  storeWord(w + gcTiPtrOffsetsPtr, offsAddr)
  storeWord(w + gcTiPtrOffsetsLen, offsLen)
  storeWord(w + gcTiNamePtr, nameAddr)
  storeWord(w + gcTiNameLen, nameLen)
  storeWord(w + gcTiMethodsPtr, 0)
  storeWord(w + gcTiMethodsLen, 0)
}

// Binds the memory the collector carves its heap out of. Separate from
// `rootInit` for the same reason `gcBindMemory` is separate from `gcInit`: the
// pages come from a platform provider this core cannot import (deviation 4).
// MUST be called before `rootInit`.
export @symbol("bit_rt_root_bind_memory")
function rootBindMemory(spanAddr: int, spanBytes: int, idxAddr: int, idxBytes: int, pageSize: int) {
  let g = ptrOf(gcState)
  gcAddr = int(g)
  infoAddr = int(ptrOf(infoBlock))
  gcBindMemory(g, spanAddr, spanBytes, idxAddr, idxBytes, pageSize)
}

// Boots the allocator, the collector and the type descriptors, in that order.
//
// THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND MATCHES `boot()` EXACTLY: the heap is
// initialized before the collector, because `gcInit` takes the heap's address
// and its two lock cells; the descriptors are written before the first
// allocation, because `gcAlloc` reads a descriptor's size to size the body.
// A "cleaner" order — descriptors first, say — happens to work today and would
// break the moment a descriptor is allocated rather than static. Reproduced
// rather than improved, per the port's standing rule.
//
// Returns false if the collector could not start; true otherwise. Calling it
// twice is refused (Zig asserts `!g_booted`; there is no such thing as
// rebooting this runtime).
export @symbol("bit_rt_root_init")
function rootInit(markStackLen: int): bool {
  if (booted != 0) {
    return false
  }
  // A drift in either width would silently truncate the block these arrays
  // stand in for. Literal lengths cannot reference the consts, so check them.
  if (gcWords != 37 || heapWords != 39) {
    return false
  }

  gcAddr = int(ptrOf(gcState))
  infoAddr = int(ptrOf(infoBlock))

  let hp = ptrOf(heapBlock)
  heapInit(hp)
  let hlk = ptrOf(heapLockCell)
  atomicStore(hlk, spinUnlocked)
  let llk = ptrOf(listLockCell)
  atomicStore(llk, spinUnlocked)

  let base = infoAddr

  // Pointer-offset arrays, written before the descriptors that name them.
  storeWord(base + riSliceOffs * 8, 0)
  storeWord(base + riMapOffs * 8 + 0, 0)
  storeWord(base + riMapOffs * 8 + 8, 8)
  storeWord(base + riMapOffs * 8 + 16, 16)

  // `string` and the two element buffers are LEAVES; the three headers carry
  // the traced offsets. Sizes are the header sizes: a string's inline bytes and
  // a buffer's words are sized per allocation via `gcAllocRaw`, exactly as the
  // Zig does with its own 0-size leaf descriptors.
  writeInfo(base, riString, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riSlice, slcHeaderSize, base + riSliceOffs * 8, 1, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riSliceBuf, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riMap, mapHeaderSize, base + riMapOffs * 8, 3, 0, 0)
  writeInfo(base, riMapCtrl, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  // The `ref_array_info` sentinel: matched by ADDRESS by the collector, never
  // by content, which is why it must be a stable module-state cell.
  writeInfo(base, riRefArray, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

  let g = ptrOf(gcState)
  if (!gcInit(g, int(hp), int(hlk), int(llk), base + riRefArray * 8, markStackLen)) {
    return false
  }
  booted = 1
  return true
}

// `BIT_GC` / `BIT_GC_STATS` equivalents. Split out because reading the
// environment needs the process entry (SEAM 3); the values are handed in.
export @symbol("bit_rt_root_configure")
function rootConfigure(enabled: bool, stress: bool, minTrigger: int, growthPct: int, verbose: bool) {
  let g = ptrOf(gcState)
  gcConfigure(g, enabled, stress, minTrigger, growthPct, verbose)
}

// Allocation failures since boot. Zero on any run that did not exhaust memory;
// see deviation 3.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_stat_oom")
function rootStatOom(): int {
  return statOom
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Allocation
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `bit_rt_gc_alloc` (ABI.md §2/§6): a zeroed body of the type `infoAddr`
// describes. 0 on exhaustion (deviation 3).
//
// Zig also registers the calling thread as a mutator here, because the
// allocation door is reached long before the first safepoint and an
// unregistered allocator is one the rendezvous does not wait for. That
// registration is SEAM 2 — it needs `World`, which is unported. On a
// single-worker runtime (ABI.md §5 pins nthreads to 1) there is exactly one
// allocating thread, so nothing is lost TODAY; wiring `World` must restore this
// call, and this comment is here so that is not forgotten.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_gc_alloc")
function rtGcAlloc(info: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let body = gcAlloc(g, info)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
  }
  return body
}

// A `len`-byte string body: the `{ptr, len}` header followed by the bytes, with
// `ptr` addressing them. Returns the object address, or 0 on exhaustion.
@nosplit function allocString(nbytes: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let body = gcAllocRaw(g, strHeaderSize + nbytes, base + riString * 8)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
    return 0
  }
  storeWord(body + strPtr, body + strHeaderSize)
  storeWord(body + strLen, nbytes)
  return body
}

// A `cap`-word element buffer. A ref buffer carries the `ref_array_info`
// descriptor so the collector traces its words as roots (ABI.md §2); a non-ref
// buffer is a leaf. `cap == 0` yields a valid zero-length body — never indexed,
// because every bounds check rejects it.
@nosplit function allocBuf(words: int, isRef: bool): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let info = base + riSliceBuf * 8
  if (isRef) {
    info = base + riRefArray * 8
  }
  let body = gcAllocRaw(g, words * 8, info)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
  }
  return body
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Strings (ABI.md §2)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Copies `n` bytes from `src` to `dst`. Ascending, and the two regions here are
// always disjoint (a fresh object and an existing one), so no overlap rule is
// needed. Bounded by `n`.
@nosplit function copyBytes(dst: int, src: int, n: int) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let b = loadByte(src + i)
    storeByte(dst + i, b)
    i = i + 1
  }
}

@nosplit function strBytes(s: int): int {
  let p = loadWord(s + strPtr)
  return p
}

@nosplit function strSize(s: int): int {
  let n = loadWord(s + strLen)
  return n
}

// `bit_rt_string_concat` (ABI.md §2): a fresh string holding `a` then `b`.
// Binary — lowering folds an N-part interpolation into a chain of these.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_concat")
function rtStringConcat(a: int, b: int): int {
  let an = strSize(a)
  let bn = strSize(b)
  let out = allocString(an + bn)
  if (out == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let dst = strBytes(out)
  copyBytes(dst, strBytes(a), an)
  copyBytes(dst + an, strBytes(b), bn)
  return out
}

// `bit_rt_string_eq` (ABI.md §2): byte-wise equality; backs string `==`/`!=`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_eq")
function rtStringEq(a: int, b: int): bool {
  let an = strSize(a)
  let bn = strSize(b)
  if (an != bn) {
    return false
  }
  let pa = strBytes(a)
  let pb = strBytes(b)
  let i = 0
  while (i < an) {
    let x = loadByte(pa + i)
    let y = loadByte(pb + i)
    if (x != y) {
      return false
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return true
}

// `bit_rt_string_byte` (ABI.md §2): the byte at `index`, backing `s[i]` (SPEC
// §12.2 — result type `u8`). Out of range is a panic in Zig; `@nosplit` cannot
// panic, so it returns -1, a value no byte can take. See deviation 3.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_byte")
function rtStringByte(s: int, index: int): int {
  let n = strSize(s)
  if (index < 0 || index >= n) {
    return -1
  }
  let b = loadByte(strBytes(s) + index)
  return b
}

// `bit_rt_string_slice` (ABI.md §2): `s[lo:hi]` (SPEC §12.6) — a fresh string
// holding bytes `[lo, hi)`. COPIES rather than sharing `s`'s buffer: a string
// header's `ptr` is an interior pointer into its own object, so a shared view
// could not keep the backing alive. Returns 0 unless `0 <= lo <= hi <= len`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_slice")
function rtStringSlice(s: int, lo: int, hi: int): int {
  let n = strSize(s)
  if (lo < 0 || lo > hi || hi > n) {
    return 0
  }
  let out = allocString(hi - lo)
  if (out == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  copyBytes(strBytes(out), strBytes(s) + lo, hi - lo)
  return out
}

// `bit_rt_string_from_int`: the decimal text of `v`.
//
// The magnitude is taken in u64, so i64's most negative value — which has no
// positive counterpart — is handled without a special case.
//
// Doing the negation in `int` instead (`u64(0 - v)`) turns out to be EXACTLY
// EQUIVALENT, not a bug: `0 - (-2^63)` wraps back to `-2^63`, whose u64 bit
// pattern is 2^63, which is the magnitude wanted. Recorded because it was
// asserted here as a hazard on first writing and mutation testing disproved it
// (M15 survived, correctly). The u64 form is kept for saying plainly what it
// means, not because the alternative is wrong.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_from_int")
function rtStringFromInt(v: int): int {
  let neg = v < 0
  let mag: u64 = u64(v)
  if (neg) {
    mag = u64(0) - u64(v)
  }

  // Count the digits first, then write them into the string from the END
  // backwards. Zig generates least-significant-first into a scratch buffer and
  // reverses; that needs an addressable local array, which Bit does not have
  // (`ptrOf` takes a slice or module state, E0053) — and a module-state scratch
  // would make this entry point non-reentrant for the sake of a buffer. Two
  // passes over at most 20 digits is the cheaper trade and needs no storage.
  let ndigits = 1
  let probe = mag / u64(10)
  // Bounded: at most 20 decimal digits fit in a u64.
  while (probe > u64(0)) {
    ndigits = ndigits + 1
    probe = probe / u64(10)
  }
  let total = ndigits
  if (neg) {
    total = total + 1
  }

  let out = allocString(total)
  if (out == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let dst = strBytes(out)
  if (neg) {
    storeByte(dst, 45)
  }
  let at = total
  let rest = mag
  // Bounded by `ndigits`, computed above from the same value.
  let k = 0
  while (k < ndigits) {
    let q = rest / u64(10)
    let r = rest - q * u64(10)
    at = at - 1
    storeByte(dst + at, 48 + int(r))
    rest = q
    k = k + 1
  }
  return out
}

// `bit_rt_string_from_bool`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_from_bool")
function rtStringFromBool(v: bool): int {
  if (v) {
    let t = allocString(4)
    if (t == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    let p = strBytes(t)
    storeByte(p + 0, 116)
    storeByte(p + 1, 114)
    storeByte(p + 2, 117)
    storeByte(p + 3, 101)
    return t
  }
  let f = allocString(5)
  if (f == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let q = strBytes(f)
  storeByte(q + 0, 102)
  storeByte(q + 1, 97)
  storeByte(q + 2, 108)
  storeByte(q + 3, 115)
  storeByte(q + 4, 101)
  return f
}

// `bit_rt_bytes_from_string` (ABI.md §2): `[]byte(s)` (SPEC §12.9) — a fresh
// `[]u8` whose element `i` is `s[i]`. Slices are WORD-stored, so each byte
// lands zero-extended in its own word and the buffer is a leaf.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_bytes_from_string")
function rtBytesFromString(s: int): int {
  let n = strSize(s)
  let h = rtSliceNew(n, n, 0)
  if (h == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let buf = loadWord(h + slcBuf)
  let src = strBytes(s)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let b = loadByte(src + i)
    storeWord(buf + i * 8, b)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return h
}

// `bit_rt_string_from_bytes` (ABI.md §2): `string(b)` for a `[]u8` (SPEC §12.9)
// — a fresh string whose byte `i` is the LOW BYTE of element word `i`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_string_from_bytes")
function rtStringFromBytes(h: int): int {
  let n = loadWord(h + slcLen)
  let off = loadWord(h + slcOff)
  let buf = loadWord(h + slcBuf)
  let out = allocString(n)
  if (out == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let dst = strBytes(out)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let w = loadWord(buf + (off + i) * 8)
    storeByte(dst + i, w & 255)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Slices (ABI.md §2)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `bit_rt_slice_new`: a fresh `[]T` of length `len`, capacity `max(len, cap)`,
// elements zeroed. Backs slice literals (`len == cap == N`) and `[]T(n[, m])`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_slice_new")
function rtSliceNew(want: int, capWant: int, isRef: int): int {
  let c = capWant
  if (c < want) {
    c = want
  }
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let h = gcAlloc(g, base + riSlice * 8)
  if (h == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
    return 0
  }
  let buf = allocBuf(c, isRef != 0)
  if (buf == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  storeWord(h + slcBuf, buf)
  storeWord(h + slcLen, want)
  storeWord(h + slcOff, 0)
  storeWord(h + slcCap, c)
  storeWord(h + slcIsRef, isRef)
  return h
}

// `bit_rt_slice_append`: appends one word, DOUBLING FROM 1 when full. A grow
// reallocates from the current view and resets `off` to 0. Mutates the header in
// place and returns it, so `s = append(s, x)` observes the new length and buffer
// through the same value — which is also why `append` aliases its argument's
// backing, a documented hazard for callers.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_slice_append")
function rtSliceAppend(h: int, word: int): int {
  let vlen = loadWord(h + slcLen)
  let vcap = loadWord(h + slcCap)
  let off = loadWord(h + slcOff)
  if (vlen == vcap) {
    let newcap = vcap * 2
    if (vcap == 0) {
      newcap = 1
    }
    let isRef = loadWord(h + slcIsRef)
    let nb = allocBuf(newcap, isRef != 0)
    if (nb == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    let old = loadWord(h + slcBuf)
    let i = 0
    while (i < vlen) {
      let w = loadWord(old + (off + i) * 8)
      storeWord(nb + i * 8, w)
      i = i + 1
    }
    storeWord(h + slcBuf, nb)
    storeWord(h + slcOff, 0)
    storeWord(h + slcCap, newcap)
    off = 0
  }
  let buf = loadWord(h + slcBuf)
  storeWord(buf + (off + vlen) * 8, word)
  storeWord(h + slcLen, vlen + 1)
  return h
}

// `bit_rt_slice_get`: bounds-checked element read. Zig panics out of range;
// see deviation 3 — `ok` is reported through `rtSliceInBounds` for a caller that
// must distinguish, and the value reads 0.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_slice_get")
function rtSliceGet(h: int, index: int): int {
  let vlen = loadWord(h + slcLen)
  if (index < 0 || index >= vlen) {
    return 0
  }
  let off = loadWord(h + slcOff)
  let buf = loadWord(h + slcBuf)
  let w = loadWord(buf + (off + index) * 8)
  return w
}

// Whether `index` is a legal element of `h`. The predicate Zig's bounds check
// panics on, exposed so the panic can be raised by the layer that owns the
// write path (SEAM 3) rather than swallowed here.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_slice_in_bounds")
function rtSliceInBounds(h: int, index: int): bool {
  let vlen = loadWord(h + slcLen)
  return index >= 0 && index < vlen
}

// `bit_rt_slice_set`: bounds-checked element write. Out of range is a no-op
// (deviation 3); `rtSliceInBounds` is the check a caller raises on.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_slice_set")
function rtSliceSet(h: int, index: int, word: int) {
  let vlen = loadWord(h + slcLen)
  if (index < 0 || index >= vlen) {
    return
  }
  let off = loadWord(h + slcOff)
  let buf = loadWord(h + slcBuf)
  storeWord(buf + (off + index) * 8, word)
}

// `bit_rt_slice_slice`: `s[lo:hi]` (SPEC §12.6). SHARES `s`'s buffer; the new
// view is `buf[off+lo .. off+hi]` with capacity extending to the old end
// (`cap - lo`, Go semantics). Returns 0 unless `0 <= lo <= hi <= cap`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_slice_slice")
function rtSliceSlice(h: int, lo: int, hi: int): int {
  let vcap = loadWord(h + slcCap)
  if (lo < 0 || lo > hi || hi > vcap) {
    return 0
  }
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let nh = gcAlloc(g, base + riSlice * 8)
  if (nh == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
    return 0
  }
  let off = loadWord(h + slcOff)
  storeWord(nh + slcBuf, loadWord(h + slcBuf))
  storeWord(nh + slcLen, hi - lo)
  storeWord(nh + slcOff, off + lo)
  storeWord(nh + slcCap, vcap - lo)
  storeWord(nh + slcIsRef, loadWord(h + slcIsRef))
  return nh
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Wyhash (final version 4) — the string-key hash
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ported from the pinned Zig stdlib's `std/hash/wyhash.zig`, which
// `root.zig`'s `mapHash` calls, and NOT from memory of the upstream C: the two
// have diverged across versions and only the exact routine the Zig calls
// produces the same slot order. `tests/stress/rootmap` checks the port against
// the SEVEN OFFICIAL TEST VECTORS from wangyi-fudan's `test_vector.cpp` that
// Zig's own unit test uses, which is an oracle independent of this codebase.
//
// Only the one-shot `hash(seed, input)` path is ported. The streaming
// `update`/`final` half exists for incremental hashing, which no map key needs.

const wySecret0: u64 = 0xa0761d6478bd642f
const wySecret1: u64 = 0xe7037ed1a0b428db
const wySecret2: u64 = 0x8ebc6af09c88c6e3
const wySecret3: u64 = 0x589965cc75374cc3

// The high 64 bits of the 128-bit product `a * b`, via 32-bit limbs.
//
// Bit has no u128 and no widening multiply, so the schoolbook decomposition is
// the only route. The carry out of the middle sum is the step that is easy to
// drop — and dropping it is invisible on small inputs, which is exactly why the
// test vectors (all four length classes) are the guard rather than a spot check.
@nosplit function mulHi(a: u64, b: u64): u64 {
  let lo32: u64 = 0xffffffff
  let a0 = a & lo32
  let a1 = a >> 32
  let b0 = b & lo32
  let b1 = b >> 32
  let p00 = a0 * b0
  let p01 = a0 * b1
  let p10 = a1 * b0
  let p11 = a1 * b1
  let mid = (p00 >> 32) + (p01 & lo32) + (p10 & lo32)
  return p11 + (p01 >> 32) + (p10 >> 32) + (mid >> 32)
}

// Zig's `mix`: the low and high halves of the 128-bit product, xored.
@nosplit function wyMix(a: u64, b: u64): u64 {
  let lo = a * b
  let hi = mulHi(a, b)
  return lo ^ hi
}

// `n` little-endian bytes at `addr`, zero-extended. `n <= 8`.
@nosplit function readLe(addr: int, n: int): u64 {
  let v: u64 = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let b = loadByte(addr + i)
    v = v | (u64(b) << u64(i * 8))
    i = i + 1
  }
  return v
}

// Wyhash over the `len` bytes at `addr`, seed 0 — the exact call `root.zig`'s
// `mapHash` makes.
//
// The state is three cells rather than a `[3]u64` because a local fixed array
// is not addressable the way module state is; the round below is unrolled over
// them, which is what Zig's `inline for (0..3)` compiles to anyway.
@nosplit function wyhash(addr: int, vlen: int, seed: u64): u64 {
  let s0 = seed ^ wyMix(seed ^ wySecret0, wySecret1)
  let s1 = s0
  let s2 = s0
  let a: u64 = 0
  let b: u64 = 0

  if (vlen <= 16) {
    // smallKey
    if (vlen >= 4) {
      let end = vlen - 4
      let quarter = (vlen >> 3) << 2
      a = (readLe(addr, 4) << 32) | readLe(addr + quarter, 4)
      b = (readLe(addr + end, 4) << 32) | readLe(addr + end - quarter, 4)
    } else if (vlen > 0) {
      let c0 = loadByte(addr)
      let c1 = loadByte(addr + (vlen >> 1))
      let c2 = loadByte(addr + vlen - 1)
      a = (u64(c0) << 16) | (u64(c1) << 8) | u64(c2)
      b = 0
    } else {
      a = 0
      b = 0
    }
  } else {
    let i = 0
    if (vlen >= 48) {
      // round(): three independent lanes per 48-byte block.
      while (i + 48 < vlen) {
        let r0 = readLe(addr + i + 0, 8)
        let r1 = readLe(addr + i + 8, 8)
        s0 = wyMix(r0 ^ wySecret1, r1 ^ s0)
        let r2 = readLe(addr + i + 16, 8)
        let r3 = readLe(addr + i + 24, 8)
        s1 = wyMix(r2 ^ wySecret2, r3 ^ s1)
        let r4 = readLe(addr + i + 32, 8)
        let r5 = readLe(addr + i + 40, 8)
        s2 = wyMix(r4 ^ wySecret3, r5 ^ s2)
        i = i + 48
      }
      // final0
      s0 = s0 ^ (s1 ^ s2)
    }
    // final1, with `start_pos = i` over the whole input.
    let j = i
    while (j + 16 < vlen) {
      let x = readLe(addr + j, 8)
      let y = readLe(addr + j + 8, 8)
      s0 = wyMix(x ^ wySecret1, y ^ s0)
      j = j + 16
    }
    a = readLe(addr + vlen - 16, 8)
    b = readLe(addr + vlen - 8, 8)
  }

  // final2
  a = a ^ wySecret1
  b = b ^ s0
  let lo = a * b
  let hi = mulHi(a, b)
  a = lo
  b = hi
  return wyMix(a ^ wySecret0 ^ u64(vlen), b ^ wySecret1)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Maps (SPEC §11.2, §13.5; ABI.md §15)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Wyhash over the bytes of a `string`, at an explicit seed. Exists so the
// official test vectors can drive the exact routine `mapHash` uses, rather than
// a copy of it that could drift.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_wyhash_string")
function rtWyhashString(s: int, seed: u64): u64 {
  let p = strBytes(s)
  let n = strSize(s)
  return wyhash(p, n, seed)
}

// splitmix64's finalizer: cheap avalanche so low-entropy integer keys (0,1,2,…)
// do not cluster in the low probe bins. Identical constants to the Zig.
@nosplit function scalarHash(key: u64): u64 {
  let x = key
  x = (x ^ (x >> 30)) * 0xbf58476d1ce4e5b9
  x = (x ^ (x >> 27)) * 0x94d049bb133111eb
  return x ^ (x >> 31)
}

// A key's hash: a `string` key hashes its BYTES (so two equal strings at
// different addresses collide correctly), a scalar hashes by value.
@nosplit function mapHash(m: int, key: int): u64 {
  if (loadWord(m + mapKeyIsString) != 0) {
    let p = strBytes(key)
    let n = strSize(key)
    return wyhash(p, n, u64(0))
  }
  return scalarHash(u64(key))
}

// Key equality, matching `mapHash`'s notion of identity.
@nosplit function mapKeyEq(m: int, a: int, b: int): bool {
  if (loadWord(m + mapKeyIsString) != 0) {
    return rtStringEq(a, b)
  }
  return a == b
}

// Locates `key`'s slot. Returns the slot index; `mapProbeFound` reports whether
// it holds the key. When it does not, the slot is where the key BELONGS — the
// first tombstone seen, else the terminating EMPTY.
//
// Split into two calls rather than a two-result return because the pair is read
// immediately and adjacently at every call site, and a struct return would be a
// traced type this `@nosplit` body cannot hold. `probeFound` is written by
// `mapProbe` and read by the very next statement, with no allocation, no
// safepoint and no call in between — the same adjacency the ABI already relies
// on for `iface_as_ok`.
let probeFound: i64 = 0

@nosplit function mapProbe(m: int, key: int): int {
  let vcap = loadWord(m + mapCap)
  let mask = vcap - 1
  let ctrl = loadWord(m + mapCtrl)
  let keys = loadWord(m + mapKeys)
  let i = int(mapHash(m, key) & u64(mask))
  let firstTomb = vcap    // sentinel: "none seen"
  let steps = 0
  // Bounded by `cap`: the 7/8 load factor guarantees an EMPTY slot exists.
  while (steps < vcap) {
    let c = loadByte(ctrl + i)
    if (c == ctrlEmpty) {
      probeFound = 0
      if (firstTomb != vcap) {
        return firstTomb
      }
      return i
    }
    if (c == ctrlTomb) {
      if (firstTomb == vcap) {
        firstTomb = i
      }
    } else {
      let k = loadWord(keys + i * 8)
      if (mapKeyEq(m, k, key)) {
        probeFound = 1
        return i
      }
    }
    i = (i + 1) & mask
    steps = steps + 1
  }
  // Unreachable: the load factor guarantees an EMPTY slot. Zig panics here;
  // reporting "not found" at the last slot is the non-panicking equivalent and
  // cannot corrupt — a caller inserting there overwrites one entry rather than
  // running off the buffer.
  probeFound = 0
  return vcap - 1
}

@nosplit function allocCtrl(vcap: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let body = gcAllocRaw(g, vcap, base + riMapCtrl * 8)
  if (body == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
  }
  // Zeroed by gcAllocRaw, so every slot starts CtrlEmpty.
  return body
}

// Doubles `cap`, rehashes every FULL slot and drops tombstones.
//
// Reinsertion probes the NEW table linearly from the key's hash and stops at the
// first non-FULL slot. It cannot encounter a tombstone (the new ctrl buffer is
// freshly zeroed), which is why this loop is simpler than `mapProbe` and still
// correct.
@nosplit function mapGrow(m: int): bool {
  let vcap = loadWord(m + mapCap)
  let newcap = vcap * 2
  let keyIsString = loadWord(m + mapKeyIsString)
  let valIsRef = loadWord(m + mapValIsRef)
  let nkeys = allocBuf(newcap, keyIsString != 0)
  if (nkeys == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let nvals = allocBuf(newcap, valIsRef != 0)
  if (nvals == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let nctrl = allocCtrl(newcap)
  if (nctrl == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let mask = newcap - 1
  let keys = loadWord(m + mapKeys)
  let vals = loadWord(m + mapVals)
  let ctrl = loadWord(m + mapCtrl)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded by the old capacity.
  while (i < vcap) {
    let c = loadByte(ctrl + i)
    if (c == ctrlFull) {
      let key = loadWord(keys + i * 8)
      let j = int(mapHash(m, key) & u64(mask))
      // Bounded: the new table is half empty, so an open slot is always near.
      while (loadByte(nctrl + j) == ctrlFull) {
        j = (j + 1) & mask
      }
      storeWord(nkeys + j * 8, key)
      storeWord(nvals + j * 8, loadWord(vals + i * 8))
      storeByte(nctrl + j, ctrlFull)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  storeWord(m + mapKeys, nkeys)
  storeWord(m + mapVals, nvals)
  storeWord(m + mapCtrl, nctrl)
  storeWord(m + mapCap, newcap)
  // Tombstones reclaimed: `used` falls back to the live count.
  storeWord(m + mapUsed, loadWord(m + mapLen))
  return true
}

// `bit_rt_map_new`: an empty map. `keyIsString`/`valIsRef` (0/1) are fixed by
// K/V at the call site and decide hashing, equality and GC tracing.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_new")
function rtMapNew(keyIsString: int, valIsRef: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let base = infoAddr
  let m = gcAlloc(g, base + riMap * 8)
  if (m == 0) {
    statOom = statOom + 1
    return 0
  }
  let keys = allocBuf(mapInitCap, keyIsString != 0)
  if (keys == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let vals = allocBuf(mapInitCap, valIsRef != 0)
  if (vals == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let ctrl = allocCtrl(mapInitCap)
  if (ctrl == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  storeWord(m + mapKeys, keys)
  storeWord(m + mapVals, vals)
  storeWord(m + mapCtrl, ctrl)
  storeWord(m + mapLen, 0)
  storeWord(m + mapCap, mapInitCap)
  storeWord(m + mapUsed, 0)
  storeWord(m + mapKeyIsString, keyIsString)
  storeWord(m + mapValIsRef, valIsRef)
  return m
}

// `bit_rt_map_set`: insert or overwrite `m[key] = val`.
//
// THE GROW TEST RUNS BEFORE THE PROBE, exactly as the Zig's does, and the order
// is observable: growing first means the slot the probe returns is a slot in the
// NEW table. Probing first and then growing would insert at a stale index.
// Reusing a TOMB keeps `used` unchanged — only an EMPTY slot is newly consumed,
// which is what makes tombstones cost capacity until the next grow rather than
// forever.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_set")
function rtMapSet(m: int, key: int, val: int) {
  if (m == 0) {
    return
  }
  let used = loadWord(m + mapUsed)
  let vcap = loadWord(m + mapCap)
  if ((used + 1) * 8 >= vcap * 7) {
    if (!mapGrow(m)) {
      return
    }
  }
  let slot = mapProbe(m, key)
  if (probeFound == 0) {
    let ctrl = loadWord(m + mapCtrl)
    if (loadByte(ctrl + slot) == ctrlEmpty) {
      storeWord(m + mapUsed, loadWord(m + mapUsed) + 1)
    }
    storeByte(ctrl + slot, ctrlFull)
    storeWord(m + mapLen, loadWord(m + mapLen) + 1)
  }
  storeWord(loadWord(m + mapKeys) + slot * 8, key)
  storeWord(loadWord(m + mapVals) + slot * 8, val)
}

// `bit_rt_map_get`: `m[key]`, or the zero word when absent (SPEC §11.2 — an
// absent key reads as V's zero value). A nil map reads as empty.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_get")
function rtMapGet(m: int, key: int): int {
  if (m == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  if (loadWord(m + mapLen) == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let slot = mapProbe(m, key)
  if (probeFound == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let v = loadWord(loadWord(m + mapVals) + slot * 8)
  return v
}

// `bit_rt_map_has`: presence test (backs the two-result form and `delete`).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_has")
function rtMapHas(m: int, key: int): bool {
  if (m == 0) {
    return false
  }
  if (loadWord(m + mapLen) == 0) {
    return false
  }
  let slot = mapProbe(m, key)
  return probeFound != 0
}

// `bit_rt_map_delete`: `delete(m, key)` — a no-op if absent.
//
// Leaves a TOMBSTONE (reclaimed at the next grow) and DROPS THE SLOT'S KEY AND
// VALUE WORDS. The drop is not tidiness: those words live in traced ref buffers,
// so leaving them would keep a removed key or value reachable and unreclaimable
// for the lifetime of the map.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_delete")
function rtMapDelete(m: int, key: int) {
  if (m == 0) {
    return
  }
  if (loadWord(m + mapLen) == 0) {
    return
  }
  let slot = mapProbe(m, key)
  if (probeFound == 0) {
    return
  }
  storeByte(loadWord(m + mapCtrl) + slot, ctrlTomb)
  storeWord(loadWord(m + mapKeys) + slot * 8, 0)
  storeWord(loadWord(m + mapVals) + slot * 8, 0)
  storeWord(m + mapLen, loadWord(m + mapLen) - 1)
}

// `bit_rt_map_len`: `len(m)` — live entry count (nil map => 0).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_len")
function rtMapLen(m: int): int {
  if (m == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let n = loadWord(m + mapLen)
  return n
}

// Iteration (`for k of m`, §13.5): `iterInit` yields the first FULL slot or -1,
// `iterNext` the next FULL slot after `prev` or -1. Slot order is unspecified
// and a concurrent insert may rehash — iteration assumes no mutation, per spec.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_iter_init")
function rtMapIterInit(m: int): int {
  return rtMapIterNext(m, -1)
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_iter_next")
function rtMapIterNext(m: int, prev: int): int {
  if (m == 0) {
    return -1
  }
  let vcap = loadWord(m + mapCap)
  let ctrl = loadWord(m + mapCtrl)
  let i = prev + 1
  while (i < vcap) {
    if (loadByte(ctrl + i) == ctrlFull) {
      return i
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return -1
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_key_at")
function rtMapKeyAt(m: int, slot: int): int {
  let k = loadWord(loadWord(m + mapKeys) + slot * 8)
  return k
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_map_val_at")
function rtMapValAt(m: int, slot: int): int {
  let v = loadWord(loadWord(m + mapVals) + slot * 8)
  return v
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Interfaces (ABI.md §2.1, §2.2)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// One method-table entry: `{id, fn_ptr}`.
const miId: int = 0
const miFn: int = 8
const miStride: int = 16

// `bit_rt_iface_lookup` (ABI.md §2.1): resolve an interface method call to a
// concrete method's code address. LINEARLY scans the receiver type's method
// table — types have few methods, so this is a short, allocation-free walk, and
// the linear order is part of the contract (a table with duplicate ids resolves
// to the first, on both implementations).
//
// The checker guarantees the receiver satisfies the interface, so a miss is a
// COMPILER BUG. Zig makes it fatal; `@nosplit` cannot, so it returns 0 — which
// faults immediately at the indirect call rather than dispatching to something
// arbitrary. Loud either way, and never silently wrong.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_iface_lookup")
function rtIfaceLookup(infoAddr: int, id: int): int {
  let base = loadWord(infoAddr + gcTiMethodsPtr)
  let n = loadWord(infoAddr + gcTiMethodsLen)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let at = base + i * miStride
    if (loadWord(at + miId) == id) {
      let f = loadWord(at + miFn)
      return f
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return 0
}

// The dynamic type of an interface value, or 0 if it does not name a live
// managed object.
//
// An interface value IS its receiver pointer (ABI.md §2.1), so its dynamic type
// is the `TypeInfo` in that object's GC header. But a nil interface is a null
// word, and a reference-typed value NEED NOT BE A GC OBJECT AT ALL — a `chan`
// is not — so the header is only safe to read once `gcOwns` confirms this
// address is exactly a live object's body. Skipping that check is the difference
// between a type assertion and reading arbitrary memory as a descriptor.
@nosplit function dynTypeOf(recv: int): int {
  if (recv == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  if (!gcOwns(g, recv)) {
    return 0
  }
  // ABI.md §1: the header sits immediately below the body, `info` at its base.
  let info = loadWord(recv - gcHeaderSize + gcHdrInfo)
  return info
}

// `bit_rt_iface_as` (ABI.md §2.2): the two-result assertion
// `let (v, ok) = iface.(T)` (SPEC §14.4). `TypeInfo`s are per concrete type, so
// DESCRIPTOR IDENTITY IS TYPE IDENTITY.
//
// NULL ON A MISMATCH IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness: the result is typed `T`, so
// handing back the un-narrowed receiver would let a caller that ignores `ok`
// read a `Square` as a `Circle`. Null is `T`'s zero value.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_iface_as")
function rtIfaceAs(recv: int, want: int): int {
  let info = dynTypeOf(recv)
  if (info == 0) {
    ifaceOk = 0
    return 0
  }
  if (info == want) {
    ifaceOk = 1
    return recv
  }
  ifaceOk = 0
  return 0
}

// `bit_rt_iface_as_ok` (ABI.md §2.2): the `ok` of the `bit_rt_iface_as`
// immediately preceding it. Lowering emits the two back to back, so no other
// assertion can land in between — see deviation 6 for why that adjacency is
// carrying more weight here than in the Zig.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_iface_as_ok")
function rtIfaceAsOk(): bool {
  return ifaceOk != 0
}

// `bit_rt_iface_assert` (ABI.md §2.2): the one-result assertion
// `let v = iface.(T)` (SPEC §14.4), which panics on a mismatch (§18.4).
//
// Returns the narrowed receiver, or 0 on a mismatch. Zig formats and raises the
// message here; that needs the write path (SEAM 3), so the SPLIT is: this
// decides, `rtIfaceAssertFailed` reports, and the caller raises. Keeping the
// decision here means the two implementations agree on WHAT fails even while
// they differ on how it is announced.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_iface_assert")
function rtIfaceAssert(recv: int, want: int): int {
  let info = dynTypeOf(recv)
  if (info != 0 && info == want) {
    return recv
  }
  return 0
}

// Whether the last `rtIfaceAssert` would have panicked. Same adjacency contract
// as `rtIfaceAsOk`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_root_iface_assert_failed")
function rtIfaceAssertFailed(recv: int, want: int): bool {
  let info = dynTypeOf(recv)
  return info == 0 || info != want
}

// The name a descriptor carries, as a fresh `string` — what
// `bit_rt_iface_assert`'s message interpolates. 0 when the descriptor is
// nameless.
export @symbol("bit_rt_root_type_name")
function rtTypeName(infoAddr: int): int {
  if (infoAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let p = loadWord(infoAddr + gcTiNamePtr)
  let n = loadWord(infoAddr + gcTiNameLen)
  let out = allocString(n)
  if (out == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  copyBytes(strBytes(out), p, n)
  return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Floats and host identity (ABI.md §14, §19)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// SEAM 6 — THE IRREDUCIBLE PRIMITIVES. `bit_rt_float_bits`,
//   `bit_rt_float32_bits`, `bit_rt_host_target`, `bit_rt_auxv`, and the whole
//   `bit_rt_floor/ceil/round/trunc/sqrt` family. **These cannot be written in
//   Bit at all**, and two of them used to live here as one-line wrappers that
//   were silently circular.
//
//   THE TRAP, because it caught this file and then very nearly caught a second
//   agent porting the math six the same way: a Bit "primitive" is NOT a
//   hardware instruction. `floatBits`, `hostTarget`, `ffloor`, `fsqrt` and the
//   rest are entries in seed/lower.zig's primitive table that lower to an
//   `ir.RtFn`, which seed/codegen/{arm64,x64}.zig's `rtSymbol` emits as **a
//   call to the very `bit_rt_*` symbol being ported**. So
//
//       export @symbol("bit_rt_root_float_bits")
//       function rtFloatBits(v: f64): u64 { return floatBits(v) }
//
//   compiles to "call bit_rt_float_bits" — the Zig — and the moment #1369 drops
//   the `_root` infix it becomes A CALL TO ITSELF. It builds, links, passes
//   every golden and every differential, and detonates as a stack overflow only
//   after the rename. `--freestanding` does not catch it either: the reference
//   IS pinned, and `refuseUnpinnedImports` checks pinning, not identity.
//
//   Both wrappers that lived here are deleted rather than "fixed" — nothing
//   called them, and there is nothing to call. A Bit body cannot express these:
//   there is no bitcast operator and, decisively, **no inverse of `floatBits`**
//   (the primitive table has `floatBits`/`float32Bits` and no `bitsToFloat`), so
//   an f64 cannot even be reconstructed from a manipulated bit pattern.
//   `hostTarget` is a compile-time property of the emit and `auxv` is a
//   process-entry fact; neither has a Bit expression at all.
//
//   THE RESOLUTION IS CODEGEN, NOT THIS FILE, and it is #1369's to make: every
//   one of these is a SINGLE INSTRUCTION on both targets (`fmov` for the
//   bitcasts, `frintm/frintp/frinta/frintz/fsqrt` on aarch64 and
//   `roundsd`/`sqrtsd` on x86-64), so the honest fix is to lower them inline the
//   way `@floor`/`@bitCast` are lowered in the Zig, which dissolves the RtFn and
//   the cycle together. Until then they stay compiler-provided.
//
//   GUARDED, not just documented: tests/rootpins.zig re-emits this module,
//   performs #1369's rename symbolically on the emitted object, and fails on any
//   definition that would then reference itself. Mutation-tested against exactly
//   the two wrappers deleted here.
